Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: Fix eMMC regulators and SPI2 power domain on Gameforce Ace

This patch fixes critical power supply configuration issues on the
Gameforce Ace handheld gaming device based on the Rockchip RK3588S SoC.

Problems fixed:

1. eMMC HS400 Mode Failure
   -------------------------
   The sdhci (eMMC) node was missing the required vmmc-supply and
   vqmmc-supply properties. This caused the following error during boot:

   [    2.320060] sdhci-dwcmshc fe2e0000.mmc: Can't reduce the clock
                          below 52MHz in HS200/HS400 mode

   Without proper regulator definitions, the eMMC controller could not
   reliably manage power states, leading to timing violations in HS400
   mode. The RK3588 power management driver requires explicit regulator
   bindings to properly configure voltage domains.

   Fix: Add vmmc-supply (3.3V main power) and vqmmc-supply (1.8V I/O)
   properties pointing to the RK806 PMIC regulators vcc_3v3_s0 and
   vcc_1v8_s0 respectively.

2. SPI2 Power Domain Integration
   ------------------------------
   The SPI2 controller (which hosts the RK806 PMIC) was not associated
   with any power domain. This caused a device link creation failure:

   [    2.129694] rockchip-pm-domain fd8d8000.power-management:
                  power-controller: Failed to create device link (0x180)
                  with supplier spi2.0 for /power-management@fd8d8000/
                  power-controller/power-domain@12

   While this warning is benign (the system continues to function),
   adding the power-domains property improves power management
   integration and may help with proper probe ordering.

   Fix: Add power-domains property referencing RK3588_PD_AUDIO, which
   is the appropriate power domain for the SPI2 controller.

Hardware context:
- SoC: Rockchip RK3588S
- PMIC: Rockchip RK806 (connected via SPI2)
- eMMC: 256GB HS400 mode
- Device: Gameforce Ace handheld gaming console

This patch follows the same pattern used in other RK3588-based devices
such as ODROID M2 and Rock 5B, which also use the RK806 PMIC.
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588s-gameforce-ace.dts | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588s-gameforce-ace.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588s-gameforce-ace.dts
index 8e8f082af..7c4c8f891 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588s-gameforce-ace.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588s-gameforce-ace.dts
@@ -1028,6 +1028,8 @@ &sdhci {
 	no-sd;
 	no-sdio;
 	non-removable;
+	vmmc-supply = <&vcc_3v3_s0>;
+	vqmmc-supply = <&vcc_1v8_s0>;
 	status = "okay";
 };

@@ -1079,6 +1081,7 @@ &spi2 {
 	pinctrl-0 = <&spi2m2_pins>, <&spi2m2_cs0>;
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	#size-cells = <0>;
+	power-domains = <&power RK3588_PD_AUDIO>;
 	status = "okay";

 	pmic@0 {
--
2.39.0
